# Python standards

## Shape

- Python ≥ 3.11, `from __future__ import annotations`, `X | None` unions.
- Line length **100** (Ruff `line-length = 100`). Prefer ≤88 when easy.
- `snake_case` modules/functions; `PascalCase` classes; relative imports inside the package.
- Module docstring on every new module (purpose in one short paragraph). "Why" comments over restating code.
- Prefer `logging` only if introducing a shared logger; today daemons use `print(..., file=sys.stderr)` — match the file you edit; do not mix both in one module.

## Fail-soft (product law)

- Panel and daemon must stay up: catch at provider/harvester/ledger edges; `# noqa: BLE001` only with a one-line why.
- **Meters / utilization:** unparsable → drop the meter or use `error_snapshot`, do **not** invent a healthy `0%`.
- **Config coercion:** invalid → documented default; include `OverflowError` in `_as_int` / `_as_float`.
- Reuse shared helpers — do not clone `_as_int`, `_parse_iso`, `_jwt_exp`, HTTP GET/POST. Put new shared coercion in one util module if none fits.

## Types and patterns

- Annotate public functions. Use `Protocol` for `Provider` / `Harvester`; keep `process_specs` a **tuple**, not a mutable list.
- Prefer `dataclasses.replace` over hand-copying snapshot fields.
- User-Agent: `tokens-go-brrr/<version>` from package version — not a hard-coded `0.1`.

## Tests

- **unittest** only (`python3 -m unittest discover -s tests`). No pytest unless the project decides to migrate.
- Fakes + temp dirs; no network in unit tests. Mirror fail-soft behaviour.
- After behaviour changes: run the relevant test module, then full suite before claiming done.

## Lint

```bash
make lint           # Ruff E/F/I — required
make format-check   # optional until a format pass lands
```

Do not mass-reformat unrelated files. Match surrounding style; use `ruff format` only on files you touch when convenient.